@meta
id: 29-03
title: Build the tags, read the manual
chapter: 29
type: lesson
skills: packadd
par: 66
minutes: 6
vscode: open the extension's marketplace page and scroll
open: retry.config.ts
vimrc: set packpath^=. | packloadall!

@brief
The dojo has shipped a third package with this lesson, `backoff`, and this
one comes with documentation:

    pack/dojo/start/backoff/doc/backoff.txt

That file is written in vim's help format — the one you have been reading
since chapter 1 every time you typed `:h`. It is not indexed yet, though.
Help topics are found through a `tags` file that sits next to the help
file, and nobody ships one, because it has to be generated on the machine
that reads it. `:helptags` generates it. Until you run it, `:h backoff`
finds nothing.

Two jobs:

  1. Generate the tags for the packages on your `runtimepath`, so
     `pack/dojo/start/backoff/doc/tags` exists.
  2. `retry.config.ts` sets `attempts: 3`, which somebody guessed. The
     plugin's help documents the right default for a payment retry. Look
     it up in the help and put that number in the config.

Change nothing in the help file itself.

@keys
| `:helptags {dir}` | build the tags file for one doc directory |
| `:helptags ALL` | do it for every doc directory on `runtimepath` |
| `:h {topic}` | open the help at a topic, once it is indexed |
| `Ctrl-]` | follow the `\|tag\|` under the cursor |
| `:q` | close the help window |

@hints
`:helptags` needs a directory. There is a form of it that finds all of
them for you, and it is spelled in capitals.
--
`:helptags ALL`. Then `:h backoff` and follow the contents, or jump
straight to the tag for the option.
--
`:helptags ALL` `Enter`, `:h g:backoff_attempts` `Enter`, read the
default, `:q` `Enter`, then fix the number in the config.

@solution
    :helptags ALL<Enter>
    :h g:backoff_attempts<Enter>   (read it)   :q<Enter>
    /attempts<Enter>  f3  r7  :wq<Enter>

`:helptags ALL` walks every `doc` directory on `runtimepath` and writes a
`tags` file into each. Look at the one it just made:

    backoff-commands	backoff.txt	/*backoff-commands*
    g:backoff_attempts	backoff.txt	/*g:backoff_attempts*

Three columns: the topic, the file, and a search command to find it. That
is the same format `ctags` produces for source code, and the same one
`Ctrl-]` uses to jump to a definition — chapter 30 leans on it. Vim has
exactly one idea of "jump to a name", and help is just another consumer
of it.

Run `:helptags ALL` after every plugin you install. Plugin managers do it
for you on update, and that is genuinely most of what they do for you.

The habit worth forming is the other half of this lesson: **read the
plugin's help before you use it.** A vim plugin with a `doc/` directory is
documented to a standard almost nothing else in your toolchain reaches —
every option, every mapping, every command, in one file, offline, in the
editor, in a format that is the same for all of them. `:h` then the plugin
name, then `Ctrl-]` down through the contents. It takes four minutes and
saves you from three months of using a quarter of a plugin.

@start pack/dojo/start/backoff/doc/backoff.txt
*backoff.txt*  Retry a failing job with exponential backoff

==============================================================================
CONTENTS                                                    *backoff-contents*

    1. Introduction ..................... |backoff-intro|
    2. Commands ......................... |backoff-commands|
    3. Options .......................... |backoff-options|

==============================================================================
1. INTRODUCTION                                                *backoff-intro*

Wraps a shell command in a retry loop with exponential backoff and jitter,
so a flaky deploy step or a rate-limited API call stops failing the build.

==============================================================================
2. COMMANDS                                                 *backoff-commands*

                                                                    *:Backoff*
:Backoff {cmd}          Run {cmd}, retrying on a non-zero exit status.

==============================================================================
3. OPTIONS                                                   *backoff-options*

                                                          *g:backoff_attempts*
g:backoff_attempts      number  (default 4)

                        How many times to try before giving up. Four is the
                        right number for a build step: with the default delay
                        it gives up after about thirty seconds.

                        Payment providers are the exception. Their advice is
                        universally seven attempts, because their own outages
                        are measured in minutes rather than seconds, and a
                        card authorisation that is abandoned early has to be
                        re-authorised by the customer.

                                                             *g:backoff_delay*
g:backoff_delay         number  (default 250)

                        Milliseconds before the first retry. Every subsequent
                        delay is double the last, plus a random jitter of up
                        to half the delay.

vim:tw=78:ts=8:ft=help:norl:

@start retry.config.ts
export const retryConfig = {
  payments: {
    attempts: 3,
    delayMs: 250,
  },
};

@expected retry.config.ts
export const retryConfig = {
  payments: {
    attempts: 7,
    delayMs: 250,
  },
};

@expected pack/dojo/start/backoff/doc/tags
:Backoff	backoff.txt	/*:Backoff*
backoff-commands	backoff.txt	/*backoff-commands*
backoff-contents	backoff.txt	/*backoff-contents*
backoff-intro	backoff.txt	/*backoff-intro*
backoff-options	backoff.txt	/*backoff-options*
backoff.txt	backoff.txt	/*backoff.txt*
g:backoff_attempts	backoff.txt	/*g:backoff_attempts*
g:backoff_delay	backoff.txt	/*g:backoff_delay*
